# What the container build must NOT see.
#
# ══ WHY THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING ═════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Two separate problems, both of which cost a build before this existed.
#
# 1. obj/ and bin/ from a Windows machine carry project.assets.json with absolute
#    Windows NuGet paths in it. Copied into a Linux container the restore appears to
#    succeed and the publish fails with "Unable to find fallback package folder
#    'C:\Program Files (x86)\...'", which reads like a broken SDK rather than a stale
#    file that should never have been there.
#
# 2. The build context is the repository ROOT, because the shared rules tree lives
#    under Assets/. Without exclusions that ships 1.2 GB of Kenney art and every
#    Unity Library artefact to the daemon on every build.

# Build output, from any machine
**/bin/
**/obj/
Tools/verify/build/

# Unity's own, none of which the server needs
Library/
Temp/
Logs/
UserSettings/
obj/
*.csproj.user
*.sln
*.slnx.user

# Art and audio. The server reads StreamingAssets and nothing else under Assets/,
# and the Dockerfile copies exactly the two directories it wants.
Assets/Kenney Game Assets All-in-1 3.7.0/
Assets/JMO Assets/
Assets/Imports/
Assets/Scenes/
Assets/Prefabs/
Assets/Materials/
Assets/Shaders/
Assets/Resources/
Assets/TextMesh Pro/
Assets/UnityTechnologies/
Assets/Fantasy Wooden GUI  Free/
Assets/*Music*/
Assets/*.meta

# The local stack's containers and volumes
server/supabase/.branches/
server/supabase/.temp/

.git/
.github/
